# STANDING GUI PLACEMENT RULE (apply on every new page/screen)

Applies to all tenant-facing and admin GUI work, alongside the existing design-system rules (no card borders, zone separation via background only, `ssot-design-system.css` only) and the desktop-poster/mobile-stacked-scroll split.

> Every screen must be laid out in the user's actual task order — first action top/left, last action bottom/end, priority controls above secondary, destructive actions isolated at the bottom — self-audited before the build is called done.

## 1. Chronological order

Lay out elements in the order the user actually performs the task. First required action → top/left. Each next step → directly below or after, in sequence. Final/completion step → bottom/end. Never place a later step above an earlier one.

## 2. Spatial priority

- High-priority, immediate-use controls → top.
- Secondary/supporting controls → middle.
- Low-frequency or destructive actions (delete, reset, wipe) → bottom, isolated from primary actions.
- Group related controls together so it's clear what belongs to which step.

## 3. Self-audit before marking a page done

Trace the eye path top-left → bottom-right. If anything needed for an earlier step sits below something needed for a later step, fix it before considering the build complete.

## 4. Scope

This rule governs placement and ordering only. It does not authorize new components, new CSS, new colors, or restyling — it governs where existing/planned elements sit relative to each other.

## Applies alongside existing conventions

- Desktop-poster vs. mobile-stacked-scroll renderer split still governs viewport-specific layout mechanics.
- This rule governs ordering/placement WITHIN whichever renderer is active — it doesn't replace the renderer split, it constrains it.

